  <div class="tray-row" data-ref="tendRow">
    <div class="tend">
      <div class="tend-head">
        <div class="tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="What to tend">
          <button role="tab" id="tab-build" data-tab="build" aria-controls="panel-build" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1" title="Lay the ground: gravel, charcoal, soil and the rest. Reshaping a living jar costs the cell's water and hurts nearby roots.">Build</button>
          <button role="tab" id="tab-land" data-tab="land" aria-controls="panel-land" aria-selected="true">Land</button>
          <button role="tab" id="tab-aquatic" data-tab="aquatic" aria-controls="panel-aquatic" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Aquatic</button>
          <button role="tab" id="tab-bugs" data-tab="bugs" aria-controls="panel-bugs" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Bugs</button>
          <button role="tab" id="tab-events" data-tab="events" aria-controls="panel-events" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Events</button>
          <button role="tab" id="tab-chaos" data-tab="chaos" aria-controls="panel-chaos" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Chaos</button>
        </div>
        <span class="level stepper" data-ref="level" hidden title="How hard the tool in hand works. The mouse wheel over the jar does the same.">
          <span class="level-label" data-ref="levelLabel">Pour</span>
          <button data-ref="levelDown" aria-label="Gentler">&minus;</button>
          <span class="level-value" data-ref="levelValue" aria-live="polite">Mid</span>
          <button data-ref="levelUp" aria-label="Stronger">+</button>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div class="tools">
      <button data-tool="water" title="Click, or click and hold, to water the soil beneath the cursor. Scroll the mouse wheel over the jar to pour gentler or harder.">Water</button>
      <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <div class="tab-panel" id="panel-build" data-panel="build" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-build" hidden>
        <button data-tool="paintGravel" title="Drainage layer: place at the bottom to catch excess water">Gravel</button>
        <button data-tool="paintCharcoal" title="Filters toxins from decaying litter: place above the gravel">Charcoal</button>
        <button data-tool="paintSoil" title="Where roots anchor and plants grow: place on top">Soil</button>
        <button data-tool="paintSand" title="Sand: ground for a succulent desert. Roots grow in it, but it drains fast and holds little water or food, which is what a succulent wants. On sand, moss grows as desert crust. A pond dug into sand soaks away, as in soil: line it with Mud.">Sand</button>
        <button data-tool="paintPeat" title="Peat: ground for a bog. It soaks up water like a sponge and stays sodden, and has next to no food in it, which is just what the carnivorous plants want and most other plants do not.">Peat</button>
        <button data-tool="paintMud" title="Watertight lining. Click inside a dug hollow and it lines the whole thing, up to the height you clicked. Nothing roots in it.">Mud</button>
        <button data-tool="dig" title="Remove a tile, leaving open space. Dig a hollow, click Mud once inside it, then fill it with water.">Dig</button>
        <span class="brush stepper" title="How many cells a Build tool paints at once. Keys: [ and ], or the mouse wheel over the jar.">
          <span class="brush-label">Brush</span>
          <button data-ref="brushDown" aria-label="Smaller brush">&minus;</button>
          <span class="brush-size" data-ref="brushSize">1</span>
          <button data-ref="brushUp" aria-label="Larger brush">+</button>
        </span>
        <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <button class="primary" data-ref="landscape" title="The whole ground in one go: gravel, charcoal and soil, shaped into hills with a pond, sand and moss as you choose, with a preview. Only before the jar has been watered or planted.">Landscape</button>
      </div>
      <div class="tab-panel" id="panel-land" data-panel="land" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-land">
        <span class="seed-group" title="Shade and damp.">
          <button data-tool="seedFern" title="Fern — shade-loving and thirsty. Keep the lamp low and the soil damp.">Fern</button>
          <button data-tool="seedFittonia" title="Fittonia — a nerve plant, with bright veins over dark leaves. Shade-loving and thirsty, like the Fern.">Fittonia</button>
          <button data-tool="seedPeperomia" title="Peperomia — thick, glossy round leaves. Shade-loving and thirsty, like the Fern.">Peperomia</button>
        </span>
        <span class="seed-group" title="Middling light and water.">
          <button data-tool="seedHerb" title="Herb — middling in everything. The forgiving one to learn a jar with.">Herb</button>
          <button data-tool="seedPilea" title="Pilea — round coin leaves on long stalks. Middling in everything, like the Herb.">Pilea</button>
        </span>
        <span class="seed-group" title="Bright and dry.">
          <button data-tool="seedSucculent" title="Succulent — wants bright light and dry soil, and rots if you keep it wet.">Succulent</button>
          <button data-tool="seedCactus" title="Tiny cactus — a spiny column with a coral flower. Wants bright light and dry soil, like the Succulent.">Tiny cactus</button>
        </span>
        <span class="seed-group" title="Bog plants: carnivorous. They want wet, poor peat and a bright lamp, and catch bugs to make up for the ground.">
          <button data-tool="flytrap" title="Venus flytrap: a rosette of toothed traps that snap shut on whatever crawls in: ants, isopods, springtails. Wants wet peat and bright light; sickens dry, or on rich soil. Grows far faster fed.">Flytrap</button>
          <button data-tool="sundew" title="Sundew: stalks tipped with glistening sticky dew that holds small fliers fast: fireflies, ladybugs, butterflies. Wants wet peat and bright light; sickens dry, or on rich soil.">Sundew</button>
          <button data-tool="pitcher" title="Pitcher plant: tall slippery pitchers that lure bees, ants and butterflies down their throats. Wants wet peat and bright light; sickens dry, or on rich soil.">Pitcher plant</button>
        </span>
        <button data-tool="seedMother" data-ref="motherSeed" class="mother-seed" title="Mother tree: a bonsai for the jar to lean on, and optional. One to a jar. She takes her look from the plants around her: a maple among shade plants, a juniper in a middling jar, a jade tree among bright dry ones. If she dies, the whole jar is thrown into upheaval for days.">Mother tree</button>
        <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <button data-tool="moss" title="Ground cover that pulls new nutrients from the air, holds moisture in, and crowds out mold. On sand it grows as desert crust instead: a dark skin of cyanobacteria and lichen that lives hot and dry, where moss cannot, and feeds a desert the same way.">Moss</button>
        <button data-tool="glowMoss" title="Glow moss: moss that glows soft green at night. Plant it like moss; it creeps through the mat beside it, cell by cell, so a little turns a whole carpet into a night-light.">Glow moss</button>
        <button data-tool="mushrooms" title="Glow mushrooms: spores for the soil. They come up on damp, shaded litter, growing by taking it up, and glow teal at night. Grown, they shed spores that start new clumps on damp litter nearby; dried out, they shrivel; in the end each clump rots back into the litter. Click on damp soil with leaf litter on it.">Glow mushrooms</button>
        <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <button data-tool="prune" title="Remove a stem or leaf; cuttings become litter, and any pests on them go too">Prune</button>
      </div>
      <div class="tab-panel" id="panel-bugs" data-panel="bugs" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-bugs" hidden>
        <button data-tool="springtails" title="Decomposers that recycle fallen litter into nutrients and fresh air">Springtails</button>
        <button data-tool="ants" title="An ant colony: a queen and a few workers. They dig a nest you can watch through the glass and forage the surface for fallen leaves, growing as the jar feeds them. Up to four colonies to a jar, each its own colour (black, red, amber, blue): rival colonies fight where they meet out foraging, and the one with more workers wins. Click on soil.">Ants</button>
        <button data-tool="ladybugs" title="The first answer to aphids. Ladybugs fly to them and eat them, and lay eggs whose larvae eat aphids too. But ants farming aphids kill any ladybug that lands on their plant: spray those with Pesticide instead. With no aphids to hunt they rest on the plants for weeks, eating flower pollen, and wake when aphids show again, so a few can look after a jar for a long while. Click anywhere in the jar.">Ladybugs</button>
        <button data-tool="isopods" title="Isopods: armoured decomposers that eat fallen leaves faster than springtails, and the bodies of dead bugs. They need cover, moss or litter or damp soil, and dry out on bare dry ground. Well fed, mothers carry broods in a pouch, but they only breed while there is litter to spare. Too many for the litter and they go hungry and chew the lowest leaves, a seedling's first. They squabble with ants on the surface, and sometimes one of them dies. Click on the soil.">Isopods</button>
        <button data-tool="butterflies" title="Butterflies, from the start: a few caterpillars set on the leaves of the plant you click by. Each eats a few leaves (never a plant's last two), then hangs itself from a stem as a chrysalis, and two or three days later a butterfly comes out. Butterflies flutter from flower to flower by day, pollinating them (they set the mother tree's fruit too), and lay eggs on the leaves that hatch into caterpillars. With no flowers they starve in a few days.">Butterflies</button>
        <button data-tool="fireflies" title="Fireflies: they rest on the leaves by day and come out at dusk to drift about the jar, blinking. In a damp jar (humidity over 45%) they lay eggs in damp soil, which hatch as glowworms that glow on the ground all night and become fireflies in about a week. A dry jar gets one generation. Geckos eat them. Click anywhere in the jar.">Fireflies</button>
        <button data-tool="geckos" title="Day geckos: a pair of little green lizards, the jar's pets. They run about on the glass as easily as the ground, bask up under the lamp when the jar is cool, and hunt bugs with a flick of the tongue: ants, ladybugs, bees, isopods, and springtails when there is nothing bigger. They sleep in cover at night. Well fed and warm, a pair lays eggs that hatch into little ones. A jar without bugs starves them in about a week. Click anywhere in the jar.">Geckos</button>
        <button data-tool="bees" title="Bees: the jar's pollinators. They fly from flower to flower, and a bloom they visit is far likelier to set seed; on the mother tree it sets fruit. They live on the flowers, so a jar with none open starves them in a couple of days, and a well-fed swarm slowly grows. Click anywhere in the jar.">Bees</button>
        <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <button data-tool="antBait" title="Ant killer: a little dish of poisoned sweet gel. Set it on the ground and foragers that smell it carry it home instead of food. The queen eats her share and dies in a day or two, and without her the nest caves in and the colony dies out. The ants that die of it are poisonous too: an isopod that eats one may sicken and die. Up to three dishes at once; each dries up in about ten days. Click on the soil.">Ant killer</button>
        <button data-tool="pesticide" title="Sprays down from the lid onto the column you click, all the way to the ground, killing every bug in its path: aphids, ants, springtails, ladybugs and isopods alike. Where it lands it leaves a green gas that creeps along the ground and rises into the plants for a while, killing anything it reaches, and it soaks a little way into the soil, so a shallow ant nest can lose its queen. Scroll the mouse wheel to spray narrower or wider. The ground it soaks and the gas settles on stays poisonous for about three days, killing the springtails, ants, isopods and ladybugs that come back, so use it where ladybugs cannot help, on aphids the ants are farming. Each plant it touches takes a dose of residue: one is harmless, but spraying the same plant again and again sickens it and can kill it.">Pesticide</button>
      </div>
      <div class="tab-panel" id="panel-events" data-panel="events" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-events" hidden>
        <button data-tool="fire" data-event title="Fire: light it on the jar and it spreads through dry plants and litter, then leaves ash that feeds the soil. Damp soil and moss slow it. Scroll the mouse wheel to choose how big, then press and hold on the jar until the ring closes. It cannot be undone.">Fire</button>
        <button data-tool="storm" data-event title="Storm: rain pours in from above, the soil floods, ponds overflow, and lightning can strike. Scroll the mouse wheel to choose how big, then press and hold on the jar until the ring closes. It cannot be undone.">Storm</button>
        <button data-tool="tornado" data-event title="Tornado: press and hold where it should touch down until the ring closes. It wanders off on a path of its own. It scours the loose soil and throws it aside, sweeps up litter, rips out small plants, strips the big ones and throws bugs. Scroll the mouse wheel to choose how big. It cannot be undone.">Tornado</button>
        <button data-tool="quake" data-event title="Earthquake: the jar shakes, slopes slump, the layers fray where they meet, fine roots tear and ant tunnels collapse; a big one brings aftershocks. Scroll the mouse wheel to choose how big, then press and hold on the jar until the ring closes. It cannot be undone.">Earthquake</button>
      </div>
      <div class="tab-panel" id="panel-chaos" data-panel="chaos" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-chaos" hidden>
        <button data-tool="meteor" title="Meteor: one click and it comes screaming in at a slant. It digs a crater with a rim, sets the ground alight, squashes bugs and throws small plants clear. Into a pond it is all splash and steam. It knocks a flying saucer out of the sky, and wakes a volcano. Click as often as you like: chaos stacks.">Meteor</button>
        <button data-tool="kaiju" title="Lizard-zilla: a day gecko grown enormous. It walks in from the far side of the jar to where you click, rears up and roars until the glass rattles, then walks off out the other side. Every footstep dents the ground, squashes ants and flattens the plants underfoot (it steps over the mother tree). It snaps a low flying saucer out of the air, and treading on a volcano sets it off.">Lizard-zilla</button>
        <button data-tool="ufo" title="Flying saucer: it hovers over where you click and beams up plants, litter, bugs and frogs, then flies off and drops the lot somewhere else. Poke it with the finger and it bolts; a meteor or a blob of lava brings it down in flames.">Saucer</button>
        <button data-tool="finger" title="Giant finger: it pokes the jar where you click. A dent in the ground, squashed ants, isopods and frogs sent flying, a few leaves knocked off on the way down. It puts out a fire, painfully. Do not poke the volcano. Or the saucer.">Finger</button>
        <button data-tool="shake" title="Snow globe: the whole jar is shaken. The litter swirls up like snow and settles anywhere, piles slump, every bug goes flying and the mother tree's fruit falls. A volcano rumbles and spits at it.">Snow globe</button>
        <button data-tool="volcano" title="Tiny volcano: the ground heaves up into a cone where you click, and it erupts, spitting lava that starts fires where it lands and boils ponds. A meteor, the finger or a shake sets it off again, harder. The cone stays.">Volcano</button>
        <button data-tool="massacre" title="Pesticide massacre: one click and two walls of poison smoke come rolling into the jar from both sides, half as tall as the jar's air, and crash together in the middle in a few seconds. Every bug in their path dies: aphids, springtails, ants, ladybugs, isopods, bees, butterflies and caterpillars, fireflies and glowworms. Only what is safe underground escapes, and the ground the fumes lay on stays poisoned for days. Every plant they reach takes a heavy dose: a healthy one is badly hurt and recovers slowly, one sprayed recently may die. The geckos and pond life are spared, though the geckos will go hungry.">Massacre</button>
      </div>
      <div class="tab-panel" id="panel-aquatic" data-panel="aquatic" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-aquatic" hidden>
        <span class="seed-group" title="Floating plants. Rivals: where two meet on the water they poison each other's edge, and one already covering a stretch of pond keeps it.">
          <button data-tool="lilies" title="Water lilies for a pond. Their broad pads spread across the surface, shading out the algae beneath and slowing evaporation, and flower once they have taken hold. Click on a pond. Rivals: Water hyacinth, Water fern.">Lily pads</button>
          <button data-tool="hyacinth" title="Water hyacinth: glossy floating rosettes with spikes of lilac flowers. Works just like lily pads. Click on a pond. Rivals: Lily pads, Water fern.">Water hyacinth</button>
          <button data-tool="waterFern" title="Water fern: chains of small fuzzy floating leaves. Works just like lily pads. Click on a pond. Rivals: Lily pads, Water hyacinth.">Water fern</button>
        </span>
        <span class="seed-group" title="Underwater plants. Rivals: where they meet they poison each other's edge.">
          <button data-tool="hornwort" title="An underwater plant for ponds. It grows up from the bottom, soaks up the food algae live on, and holds them back. A thick bed of lily pads shades it out. Click on a pond. Rival: Eelgrass.">Hornwort</button>
          <button data-tool="eelgrass" title="Eelgrass: long ribbon leaves swaying up from the pond floor. Works just like hornwort. Click on a pond. Rival: Hornwort.">Eelgrass</button>
        </span>
        <button data-tool="reeds" title="Reeds for the edges of a pond: its outermost columns, or the bank one tile out. Tall stems stand up out of the water; they soak up the pond's food, but pump its water up into the air, so a reedbed dries a pond faster and makes the jar more humid. Click in a pond to plant both sides, or at one edge for just that side.">Reeds</button>
        <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <button data-tool="fish" title="Small pond fish. They eat algae and clean up the floor, and their waste feeds the water. A pond kept spotless leaves them hungry, and stale water or a dried-up pond kills them. Click on a pond.">Fish</button>
        <button data-tool="snails" title="Ramshorn snails for a pond. They graze algae and clean up what sinks, but a pond left to go stale will kill them. Click on a pond.">Snails</button>
        <button data-tool="axolotl" title="Axolotl: a smiling salamander that walks the pond floor on little legs, swimming up for a gulp of air now and then. It eats snails and shrimp and can go weeks between meals, but it wants the water COOL: keep the jar under 25°C or it wears down, and a dried-up pond kills it. Up to three to a jar. Click on a pond.">Axolotl</button>
        <button data-tool="shrimp" title="Cherry shrimp: tiny red shrimp that pick the green off the water and the litter off the pond floor, and breed when well fed. Axolotls eat them. Click on a pond.">Shrimp</button>
        <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <button data-tool="clearPond" title="Kill every plant in a pond: its lily pads, hornwort, reeds and algae, edge to edge. Fish and snails are spared. The dead plants rot where they fall, which feeds the water, so algae can come back, and a thick bloom killed all at once sours the water. Click on a pond.">Clear pond</button>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
